CAT = "arrtCare"; SEG = "CAR"
Q = [
("A radiographer images the wrong patient after skipping the two-identifier check. Which legal term fits?",
 "Negligence", ["Battery", "Slander", "Defamation"],
 "Failing to meet the standard of care (verifying identity with two identifiers) and causing harm is negligence. Battery is unconsented touching."),
("A patient refuses a chest projection, but the radiographer performs it anyway. Which tort is this?",
 "Battery", ["Libel", "Invasion of privacy", "False imprisonment"],
 "Touching or treating a patient against their stated refusal is battery. A competent patient may withdraw consent at any time."),
("A patient rolls up a sleeve and holds out an arm for an IV start. What kind of consent is this?",
 "Implied consent", ["Informed consent", "Written consent", "Proxy consent"],
 "Consent shown by action rather than words is implied consent. Invasive or higher-risk procedures need informed consent, usually written."),
("Which element is required for valid informed consent?",
 "Risks and options explained", ["A witness who is a physician", "A signature from next of kin", "Payment arranged in advance first"],
 "Informed consent needs the patient to understand the procedure, its risks, benefits and alternatives, and to agree voluntarily while competent."),
("Under respondeat superior, who is liable for a staff radiographer's negligent act at work?",
 "The employer", ["The referring physician", "The radiologist on duty", "Only the radiographer"],
 "Respondeat superior (let the master answer) holds the employer liable for an employee's acts within the scope of employment. The radiographer may still be named too."),
("A sponge is left inside a patient after surgery. Which doctrine lets the injury itself prove negligence?",
 "Res ipsa loquitur", ["Respondeat superior", "Corporate negligence", "Borrowed servant"],
 "Res ipsa loquitur (the thing speaks for itself) applies when the injury could not happen without negligence, such as a retained surgical item."),
("A technologist uses electronic masking to hide anatomy that was on the image. What is the concern?",
 "Altering the legal record", ["Lowering the patient dose", "Changing the DICOM header", "Reducing spatial resolution"],
 "Cropping or masking anatomy that was exposed removes clinical information from the legal record. Collimate during the exposure instead."),
("Which part of the ARRT Standards of Ethics is enforceable?",
 "The Rules of Ethics", ["The Code of Ethics", "The Practice Standards", "The Patient Care Partnership"],
 "The Code of Ethics is aspirational; the Rules of Ethics set mandatory standards whose violation can lead to sanctions."),
("Before IV contrast, which adult serum creatinine result falls in the usual normal range?",
 "0.9 mg/dL", ["2.8 mg/dL", "4.5 mg/dL", "0.1 mg/dL"],
 "Adult serum creatinine is normally about 0.6 to 1.2 mg/dL. Raised values point to reduced kidney function, which is checked (with eGFR) before iodinated contrast."),
("A patient arrives on oxygen at 2 L/min by nasal cannula. What should the radiographer do with it during the procedure?",
 "Keep it running as ordered", ["Turn it off for the exposure", "Raise it to 10 L/min", "Remove the cannula to position"],
 "Oxygen is a drug given by order. The radiographer keeps the ordered flow running throughout the procedure and does not change or stop it without a physician's order."),
("A pulse oximeter reads 87% on room air. What does this suggest?",
 "Low blood oxygen", ["Normal oxygen saturation", "A rising fever", "Mild dehydration only"],
 "Normal saturation is about 95% to 100%. A reading in the 80s indicates low blood oxygen; report it and follow the oxygen orders."),
("How should a urinary drainage bag be placed while moving a patient to the table?",
 "Below the bladder", ["On the patient's abdomen", "Level with the shoulders", "Clamped on the IV pole"],
 "Keeping the bag below the bladder lets urine drain and prevents backflow, which can cause a urinary tract infection."),
("Where should an IV bag hang during a radiographic procedure?",
 "18 to 24 inches above the vein", ["Level with the patient's vein", "Below the level of the table", "6 inches above the patient"],
 "The bag should be about 18 to 24 inches (45 to 60 cm) above the vein so gravity keeps the fluid flowing without backflow of blood."),
("When helping a patient with one-sided weakness from a wheelchair to the table, the radiographer should:",
 "Lead with the stronger side", ["Lead with the weaker side", "Lift the patient alone", "Remove the gait belt first"],
 "Moving toward the patient's strong side lets them bear weight and pivot safely. A gait belt gives a secure hold."),
("Which body mechanics rule reduces back strain when lifting?",
 "Hold the load close", ["Bend at the waist", "Twist while lifting", "Keep feet together"],
 "Holding the load close, with a wide base of support and lifting with the legs, keeps the center of gravity over the feet."),
("Which link in the chain of infection does a contaminated table pad represent?",
 "Vehicle (fomite)", ["Vector", "Susceptible host", "Portal of exit"],
 "An inanimate object that carries pathogens is a fomite, an indirect vehicle-borne route of transmission. A vector is a living carrier such as a mosquito."),
("What is the most effective single way to prevent the spread of infection?",
 "Hand hygiene", ["Wearing a lead apron", "Changing linens daily", "Using a sterile field"],
 "Hand hygiene before and after every patient contact is the single most important infection control practice."),
("Two radiographers do a mobile exam on a patient in isolation. How do they usually divide the work?",
 "One touches the patient, one the unit", ["Both position the patient", "Both stay outside the room", "One works, one records dose"],
 "In the clean and dirty method one radiographer handles the patient and the covered image receptor, while the other operates the mobile unit and touches nothing contaminated."),
("A patient has Clostridioides difficile. How should staff clean their hands after care?",
 "Soap and water", ["Alcohol rub only", "Dry paper towels", "Antiseptic wipes only"],
 "C. difficile spores resist alcohol, so soap and water with friction is needed after removing gloves."),
("Neutropenic (reverse) isolation is used for a patient who:",
 "Has a weak immune system", ["Has an active MRSA wound", "Has an active measles rash", "Has a draining abscess"],
 "Reverse isolation protects a patient with low white cell counts (such as after chemotherapy) from pathogens carried by staff and visitors."),
("Which action breaks sterile technique?",
 "Reaching over the sterile field", ["Facing the sterile field", "Keeping hands above the waist", "Opening packs away from you"],
 "Reaching over a sterile field can drop contaminants onto it. Staff stay facing it, with hands above the waist."),
("Wiping the x-ray table with disinfectant between patients is an example of:",
 "Medical asepsis", ["Surgical asepsis", "Sterilization", "Reverse isolation"],
 "Medical asepsis reduces the number and spread of microorganisms, as when cleaning surfaces between patients. Surgical asepsis and sterilization remove all of them."),
("Which lab value is used most often to judge kidney function before iodinated contrast?",
 "eGFR", ["Hemoglobin", "Platelet count", "Prothrombin time"],
 "Estimated glomerular filtration rate (with creatinine and BUN) shows how well the kidneys will clear iodinated contrast."),
("A patient who takes metformin has severe kidney disease (eGFR below 30) and receives IV iodinated contrast. What is usually done with the metformin?",
 "Held for 48 hours after", ["Doubled the night before", "Given with the contrast", "Stopped for one year"],
 "Under ACR guidance, metformin is held at the time of and for 48 hours after contrast in patients with an eGFR below 30 or acute kidney injury, and restarted once kidney function is rechecked, because of the risk of lactic acidosis."),
("Compared with ionic iodinated contrast, nonionic contrast has:",
 "Lower osmolality", ["Higher osmolality", "More free ions", "A higher reaction rate"],
 "Nonionic agents do not dissociate into ions, so their osmolality is closer to blood and adverse reactions are less frequent."),
("After an iodinated contrast injection, a patient develops a few hives and mild itching. This is a:",
 "Mild reaction", ["Severe reaction", "Vasovagal response", "Local effect only"],
 "Limited urticaria and itching are mild systemic reactions; monitor the patient and notify the radiologist. Airway swelling or hypotension would be severe."),
("Contrast leaks into the tissue around an IV site. What is this called?",
 "Extravasation", ["Phlebitis", "Air embolism", "Thrombosis"],
 "Extravasation (infiltration) is contrast escaping from the vein into surrounding tissue. Stop the injection, elevate the limb and notify the radiologist."),
("Which drug is the first-line treatment for anaphylaxis?",
 "Epinephrine", ["Diphenhydramine", "Atropine", "Nitroglycerin"],
 "Epinephrine reverses the bronchospasm and hypotension of anaphylaxis. Antihistamines such as diphenhydramine treat milder symptoms."),
("A diabetic patient waiting for a fasting study becomes shaky, sweaty and confused. What is most likely?",
 "Hypoglycemia", ["Hyperglycemia", "Vasovagal syncope", "Contrast extravasation"],
 "Sweating, shakiness and confusion after fasting point to low blood sugar. Give oral glucose if the patient is alert and get help."),
("A trauma patient arrives on a backboard in a cervical collar for a cross-table lateral spine. What should be done with the collar?",
 "Leave it in place", ["Remove it first", "Loosen it to turn the head", "Swap it for a pillow"],
 "The collar stays on and the head is not moved until a physician has cleared the cervical spine on the images. The cross-table lateral is taken without moving the patient."),
("A child is scared of the x-ray room. Which approach fits best?",
 "Explain simply and honestly", ["Promise it will not hurt at all", "Start before explaining anything", "Speak only to the parent"],
 "Simple, honest explanations at the child's level build trust. False promises damage cooperation for the rest of the study."),
("A patient with a new cancer diagnosis says the scan must be wrong. Which stage of grief is this?",
 "Denial", ["Bargaining", "Acceptance", "Depression"],
 "Refusing to believe the news is denial, the first of the Kubler-Ross stages. Respond calmly and refer clinical questions to the physician."),
]
